From: BC-250 Mesa patchset <noreply@github.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ac/gpu_info: enable accelerated integer dot product on
 GFX1013 (AMD BC-250)

The AMD BC-250 mining APU uses the PS5 "Oberon" die (CHIP_GFX1013), an
RDNA2-class GPU. Mesa excluded GFX1013 from has_accelerated_dot_product,
grouping it with RDNA1 (NAVI10) under the comment "GFX1013 is GFX10 plus
ray tracing instructions".

The hardware actually executes the RDNA2 packed int8 dot instructions
correctly. With the exclusion removed, RADV advertises
VK_KHR_shader_integer_dot_product and ACO emits the accelerated dot
opcodes. Verified on a real BC-250 (RADV GFX1013):

  - Vulkan MUL_MAT_ID correctness tests pass (CPU reference match).
  - MoE expert matvec (q4_K, n=1) runs ~4.3x faster:
    86.16 us -> 20.14 us (292 GFLOPS -> 1.25 TFLOPS).
  - llama.cpp flint MoE prompt processing +30% (662 -> 862 tok/s).

A software fallback would not produce a speedup, so this confirms a real
hardware instruction rather than emulation.

Signed-off-by: BC-250 Mesa patchset <noreply@github.com>
---
 src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c
+++ b/src/amd/common/ac_gpu_info.c
@@ -346,7 +346,7 @@
    out->has_unpacked_d16 = info->gfx_level == GFX8 && info->family != CHIP_STONEY;
    out->has_accelerated_dot_product =
       info->family == CHIP_VEGA20 ||
-      (info->family >= CHIP_MI100 && info->family != CHIP_NAVI10 && info->family != CHIP_GFX1013);
+      (info->family >= CHIP_MI100 && info->family != CHIP_NAVI10); /* BC-250: enable GFX1013 int dot */
    /* GFX1013 is GFX10 plus ray tracing instructions */
    out->has_image_bvh_intersect_ray = info->gfx_level >= GFX10_3 || info->family == CHIP_GFX1013;
 
